{
  "module": "modulo05-ferramentas-de-IA-para-UI-UX/modulo-03/cfp-platform",
  "concepts": [
    "full-stack-monorepo",
    "component-framework",
    "web-accessibility",
    "rest-api",
    "schema-validation",
    "layered-architecture",
    "coding-agent-harness"
  ],
  "recorded": "Original run from a copy with Nx 22.6.3, NestJS 11.1.17, Angular 21.2.6, and Node.js 26, installed with npm install because npm ci rejects the lock file as out of sync, and driven in headless Google Chrome. Each case names its program. The api ran with npx nx serve api --inspect=false, which builds it with webpack and serves it on PORT, and the frontend ran alone with npx nx run frontend:serve --excludeTaskDependencies --port=$PORT --host=127.0.0.1 --proxyConfig=frontend/proxy.conf.mjs, with Nx's daemon, cloud, and terminal UI off. The original proxy configuration hard-codes http://localhost:3000, so the copy gained proxy.conf.mjs, which sends /api to API_URL, its only patch. The frontend cases set API_URL to the replay, which stands in for the api with bodies recorded from the module's own api, the empty 500 the dev server's proxy answered while the api was not running, and NestJS's body for an unexpected error, synthesized, and it holds some answers for 1.5 seconds so the cases can read the loading states. Ids are random, so the api cases check their shape and compare later lists with the ids the api returned. Validation messages are class-validator's, so the api cases check one message per broken rule, each naming its field in declaration order, while the frontend cases compare the replayed messages exactly. Every case passed in each of 3 runs.",
  "prepare": {
    "files": ["frontend/proxy.conf.mjs"],
    "setup": ["npm install --no-audit --no-fund"]
  },
  "originals": [
    "export NX_DAEMON=false NX_NO_CLOUD=true NX_TUI=false; if [ {program} = api ]; then npx nx serve api --inspect=false; else npx nx run frontend:serve --excludeTaskDependencies --port=$PORT --host=127.0.0.1 --proxyConfig=frontend/proxy.conf.mjs; fi"
  ],
